ode keys follow encounter order, not lexical or numeric sorting. This is semantic evaluation order, not a change to JSON object serialization/canonical encoding.\n\nMake the compatibility exception explicit: formerly order-dependent admitted programs now have the single specified outcome, while pure order-independent outputs and existing refusals remain. The supplied direct-bind case must consistently yield a=1,b=1; cover reversed source-pair order, repeated/grouped keys, duplicate-expression collisions, nested scopes, empty/reduce cases and keys whose lexical/numeric order differs from encounter order. Do not broaden callable/syntactic admission or change the settled nineteen-builtin policy. Choose a simple bounded implementation strategy in the later I7 work; any encounter-order bookkeeping, group accumulation and traversal is charged before its work/allocation. Error choice, work/allocation counts and failure boundary must be deterministic too. Require order-sensitive positive controls and omission controls that intentionally reverse/shuffle value traversal or remove order tracking, and remove relevant charges to expose actual extra work/allocation. Repeating a randomized Go-map test without observing divergence is not proof of determinism; changing only serialized field order is not a fix. Cover every admitted group path that can affect semantic order or budget failure without replacing the evaluator contract with a mere refusal.\n\nRequire stored-program revalidation before replay writes; refusal leaves projection/frontier unchanged. Account for changed semantics and corpus in the existing I7 core.jsonata/grammar/dialect/identity versions wherever affected, retaining exactly ten components, historical identity recognition, stored-identity refusal and separate acknowledged reset/activation. No extra identity component or automatic reset/migration.\n\nPublish the exact amendment artifact with source/evidence previews and an ordinary adoption proposal for Hugh. I 